gcc: link with -z now by default (full RELRO)

--enable-default-pie output already gets PT_GNU_RELRO, but only partial
RELRO: the GOT stays writable for lazy binding. musl never binds lazily --
its dynamic linker resolves every PLT/GOT slot at load time -- so -z now
costs nothing at startup on this toolchain and only buys the protection:
BIND_NOW lets the loader make the whole GOT read-only after relocation.
Static executables (no dynamic section) are unaffected; -r links work.

Placed before the user's %{z}/-Wl options in LINK_COMMAND_SPEC, so an
explicit `-z lazy` / `-Wl,-z,lazy` still overrides it (last one wins).
Same idea as Alpine aports gcc 0003; written against our tree.

--- a/gcc/gcc.cc	2026-09-24 20:20:54.665903884 +0200
+++ b/gcc/gcc.cc	2026-09-24 20:21:09.320237616 +0200
@@ -1197,6 +1197,7 @@
    "%{flto|flto=*:%<fcompare-debug*} \
     %{flto} %{fno-lto} %{flto=*} %l " LINK_PIE_SPEC \
    "%{fuse-ld=*:-fuse-ld=%*} " LINK_COMPRESS_DEBUG_SPEC \
+   "-z now " \
    "%X %{o*} %{e*} %{N} %{n} %{r}\
     %{s} %{t} %{u*} %{z} %{Z} %{!nostdlib:%{!r:%{!nostartfiles:%S}}} \
     %{static|no-pie|static-pie:} %@{L*} %(link_libgcc) " \
